course: dictionary
lesson: 8616
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The same word can mean something quite different in another sentence. Context tells you which meaning is right. «Cinemas can have relaxed screenings on Sunday mornings.» Cinemas show films, so here a screening is a showing of a film.
explain: Now read this: «The nurse booked my eye screening at school.» Screening is not a film here. The words nurse, eye and school are the clues, and they point to a test. Ask yourself: does my meaning fit the words around it?
explain: You can check a meaning by putting it back into the sentence. «Security screening at the airport took a long time.» Screening here means checking people or things to see if they are safe. The meaning changes with the sentence, but the clues always show you the way.
fact: «Screening» can be a noun meaning a showing of a film or TV programme.
fact: In health, a screening is a medical test given to many people to find illnesses early, before they cause problems.
fact: In safety, screening means checking people or things to see if they are suitable or safe, such as bags at an airport.
fact: «Screen» can also be a verb. Screening as a verb means hiding or protecting something with a screen.
fact: In England, the Year 1 phonics screening check asks a child to read 40 words to a teacher, some real and some made-up.
exercise: «The Year 1 phonics screening check asks a child to read 40 words to a teacher.» What kind of screening is this? | Hiding a room from the sun | Checking bags at an airport | A showing of a film | *A test to find out how well a child reads
exercise: «Her health screening at the clinic took only ten minutes.» What does screening mean here? | *A medical test to find illnesses early | Hiding behind a screen | A film shown on a big screen | A security check at the gate
exercise: «The thick curtain was screening the window from the bright sun.» What does screening mean here? | A showing of a film | A medical test | Checking people to see if they are safe | *Hiding or protecting with a screen
test: «Security screening at the airport took a long time.» What does screening mean? | A showing of a film | A medical test | *Checking people or things to see if they are safe | Hiding with a screen
test: «Some cinemas hold relaxed screenings, with quieter sound and the lights not fully off.» What is a relaxed screening? | A computer game | A test for hearing | *A showing of a film with quieter sound | A school lesson about screens
exam: Ruby reads a text about babies: «Babies have hearing screening soon after they are born.» Which meaning fits best? | Hiding a baby behind a screen | A quiet walk in the park | *A medical test to find problems early | A film shown for babies
